From 7b1c4b6bf087f75231329f6ba82c2410909f5e34 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Semih Baskan <strst.gs@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 07:32:40 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] ARM: dts: BCM5301X: RT-N18U: add the USB VBUS switch

The RT-N18U switches USB VBUS with GPIO 13, active high, but the DT does
not describe it, so the ports keep the power the bootloader gave them.
After a power cycle, some USB 3.0 sticks in the USB 3.0 port come up on
the EHCI at USB 2.0 speed and stay there.

Describe the switch as a fixed regulator without regulator-boot-on and
make it the vbus-supply of both USB cores, as on the R7000 and R8000,
since GPIO 13 feeds both ports.

Tested on an RT-N18U with a SanDisk 3.2Gen1 stick in the USB 3.0 port.
Without this change the stick stayed at USB 2.0 after 4 of 5 power
cycles. With it, the stick came up at SuperSpeed on every power cycle
and reboot, and a stick in the USB 2.0 port enumerated after a reboot.

Signed-off-by: Semih Baskan <strst.gs@gmail.com>
---
 .../boot/dts/broadcom/bcm47081-asus-rt-n18u.dts | 17 +++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 17 insertions(+)

--- a/arch/arm/boot/dts/broadcom/bcm47081-asus-rt-n18u.dts
+++ b/arch/arm/boot/dts/broadcom/bcm47081-asus-rt-n18u.dts
@@ -72,6 +72,23 @@
 			gpios = <&chipcommon 11 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
 		};
 	};
+
+	usb_power: regulator-usb {
+		compatible = "regulator-fixed";
+		regulator-name = "usb-vcc";
+		regulator-min-microvolt = <5000000>;
+		regulator-max-microvolt = <5000000>;
+		gpios = <&chipcommon 13 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+		enable-active-high;
+	};
+};
+
+&usb2 {
+	vbus-supply = <&usb_power>;
+};
+
+&usb3 {
+	vbus-supply = <&usb_power>;
 };
 
 &usb3_phy {
